!BCPL -- BCPL on the BOX

New BCPL (MacBCPL's dialect: floats, lists, VALOF, SWITCHON, MANIFEST,
STATIC, GET, modules) compiled to machine code in memory by LLVM and run
at once, inside the box.  The compiler is MacBCPL's.

Files
  &A78 BCPL   a BCPL program or module (.bcl)
  Double-click a program to run it: its output appears in a TaskWindow;
  a program that makes a Declaritive window runs as a task of its own.
  GET "name" looks beside the program (name, name/bcl), then in the
  library.

The command
  *BCPL [-c] [-t] [-O0..3] <file>
    -c       compile only and report errors as  file:line: error: text
    -t       give the compile time
    -O0..-O3 LLVM's optimisation (default -O2)
  Escape stops a program.  A fault ends the run, not the box.

The box's builtins
  SWIs     SYS(n, r) and SYSN(name, r) call a SWI with the registers
           r!0..r!9 (r a VEC 9), in and out; an error ends the program.
           XSYS and XSYSN return the error block (0 if none), SYSFLAGS()
           gives N Z C V.  SWINUMBER(name), ERRNUM(e), ERRTEXT(e),
           CSTR(a) (a string from a RISC OS address), ADDR(p) (checked).
           Strings, vectors (VEC, GETVEC) and statics are in a dynamic
           area ("BCPL"): their addresses can be given to SWIs as they are.
  Files    FINDINPUT(name), FINDOUTPUT(name), FINDAPPEND(name),
           SELECTINPUT(s), SELECTOUTPUT(s), INPUT(), OUTPUT(), RDCH(),
           WRCH(c), ENDREAD(), ENDWRITE(), ENDSTREAM(s); RDCH gives
           ENDSTREAMCH (GET "libhdr") at the end.  With a stream
           selected, WRITES, WRITEF and the rest write to it.
  STOP(n)  ends the program with return code n (FINISH: 0).

The library (in the ROM, /usr/lib/bcpl; a module is linked when a
program calls one of its routines, module_routine)
  riscos       riscos_write0, riscos_vdu, riscos_time, riscos_cli,
               riscos_inkey, riscos_mode, riscos_cls, riscos_tab,
               riscos_colour, riscos_plot, riscos_getenv
  declaritive  desktop windows described in JSON (the Declaritive
               module): init, add, create, poll, set, get, is, closedown
  maths, geom  small examples of modules; libhdr (ENDSTREAMCH)

Not on the box
  Cocoa, Objective-C and CLASS objects, inline x86 assembler, iGui,
  sound, and building executables (programs are always compiled and run).

Examples
  Hello, SWIs, Files, Greeter (a Declaritive window), Bench (fib(30) and
  a sieve), Mandelbrot.
